 ## Specimen preparation, staining, and image acquisition
 
-Each microscopy specimen identifier represented one individual animal; multiple images and measured objects from the same specimen were repeated observations rather than independent biological replicates. For specimens sampled directly, erythrocytes were collected from an incision through the heart with a pipette and deposited at the center of a microscope slide. Slides were stained with Feulgen-Schiff reagent following Hardie et al. (2002). All images retained for morphometry and IOD were acquired by brightfield microscopy on an Olympus APX100 with a 40× objective. Slides were also reimaged with a 40× oil-immersion objective, but those images were excluded because their quality was poor.
+Each microscopy specimen identifier denoted one animal. Multiple images and measured objects from that animal were repeated observations, not independent biological replicates. For specimens sampled directly, erythrocytes were collected with a pipette from an incision through the heart and deposited at the center of a microscope slide. Slides were stained with Feulgen-Schiff reagent following Hardie et al. (2002). All retained unknown slides and the two designated *D. fuscus* standard slides, from specimens 32469 and 32470, were stained together in one Feulgen run; the staining date remains unknown. The retained standard images were acquired at 40× by brightfield microscopy without immersion oil, using the green channel. The standard slides underwent the same fixation protocol as the other slides, although this does not establish identical prior preservation histories. All images used for morphometry and IOD were acquired by brightfield microscopy on an Olympus APX100 with a 40× objective. Subsequent images acquired with a 40× oil-immersion objective were excluded because their quality was poor.
 
